# kose-scraper — credentials and defaults
#
# Copy to `.env` (same directory as the scripts) and fill in what you need:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is gitignored. Nothing here ever reaches a command line, which is
# the point: a secret in argv is readable by anything that can run `ps` and
# lands in your shell history.
#
# Precedence, highest first:
#     an explicit flag  ->  an exported environment variable  ->  .env
#     ->  the flag's default
#
# So a value exported in your shell, by direnv or by CI is never clobbered
# by a file someone forgot to delete, and a flag you typed always wins.
#
# Check what was picked up, without printing secrets:
#
#     python3 env_config.py
#
# A placeholder left as it ships below — anything still carrying `{...}` —
# is treated as UNSET rather than sent to the API as if it were a key.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# READ THIS FIRST: on Maison KOSE you need none of them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Measured 2026-09-18 from a datacentre address (netcup, Nuremberg), with no
# key, no proxy and no browser at all:
#
#   curl on a category listing            HTTP 200, 90,310 bytes
#   the same URL as python-requests/2.31  HTTP 200, 90,310 bytes  (identical)
#   the same URL with NO User-Agent       HTTP 200, 90,310 bytes  (identical)
#   12 pages back to back, no delay       12 x HTTP 200
#   a 3-page Playwright run               72 products, exit 0
#
# and zero captcha markers of any vendor across 13 captures. The site does
# not gate its catalogue by address, by User-Agent or by rate, so the honest
# answer is that the paid products buy you nothing here TODAY.
#
# What they would buy you if that changes, or at volume:
#
#   TWOCAPTCHA_KEY   solving, if Kose ever renders a challenge. Nothing in
#                    this repo claims a captcha here is unsolvable — there is
#                    simply none to solve at the moment.
#   KOSE_PROXY       many addresses, for a run large enough that one address
#                    would be conspicuous.
#   KOSE_CDP_ENDPOINT  the Scraping Browser API: a managed browser with a
#                    persistent profile and no local Chromium to maintain.


# 2captcha.com API key. One key, four separately billed products: captcha
# solving, the Scraping Browser API, proxies and fingerprints.
TWOCAPTCHA_KEY=your_2captcha_api_key_here

# The Scraping Browser API endpoint, as a CDP WebSocket URL.
#
# `country-` picks the exit country; `pid-` is a profile with persistent
# cookies and allows ONE live connection, so reuse pids rather than minting
# one per run and do not raise --concurrency over a single endpoint.
#
# NOTE these credentials expire in about a day, so treat the line below as a
# SHAPE and fetch fresh ones rather than pasting a working endpoint anywhere
# it will be read tomorrow.
KOSE_CDP_ENDPOINT=ws://{login}-zone-scraping_browser-country-jp-pid-{profileId}:{password}@cb.2captcha.com:9222

# A proxy for the browser to use. Credentials go through the driver's own
# fields, never onto a command line.
#
# `jp` is the exit this site is written for — it is a Japanese storefront in
# Japanese with JPY prices — but it is not required: a German datacentre
# address is served identically, which is the measurement above.
KOSE_PROXY=http://{user}:{password}@jp.proxy.2captcha.com:2334

# A default URL, so a run needs no --url. An explicit flag still wins.
KOSE_URL=https://maison.kose.co.jp/site/cosmedecorte/c/c15/
